NAME
       math - floating point functions

SYNOPSIS
       #include <math.h>

       double sqrt(double x);
       double pow(double x, double y);
       double exp(double x);   double log(double x);
       double sin(double x);   double cos(double x);
       double tan(double x);   double atan2(double y, double x);
       double floor(double x); double ceil(double x);
       double fabs(double x);  double fmod(double x, double y);
       double round(double x); double ldexp(double x, int e);
       double log2(double x);  double pow10(int e);
       long   lrint(double x);

       float  sqrtf(float x);  float  fabsf(float x);
       float  sinf(float x);   float  cosf(float x);
       float  floorf(float x); float  ceilf(float x);
       long   lrintf(float x);

       int    isnan(double x); int    isinf(double x);

DESCRIPTION
       The usual double-precision functions, with single-precision
       variants where the smaller type is enough.

       isnan and isinf are the way to test a result: a NaN compares
       unequal to everything including itself, so x != x is true but
       reads as a mistake.

       Userspace is compiled with SSE, so float and double work
       normally. The kernel saves and restores the SSE state on every
       context switch and starts each task with the control words at
       their standard values, so arithmetic that would raise a masked
       exception behaves as it does everywhere else instead of faulting.

SEE ALSO
       calc(1), image(3)
